---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
  "itemListElement": [
    { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 1, "name": "Home", "item": new URL("/", Astro.site).toString() },
    { "@type": "ListItem", "position": 2, "name": "About & Methodology", "item": new URL("/about", Astro.site).toString() }
  ]
};
---

<Layout
  title="About & Methodology"
  description="How the Executive Orders Archive sources its data and computes reading time, readability grade, and constitutional sentiment scoring."
  structuredData={breadcrumbJsonLd}
>
  <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-10">

    <nav class="flex items-center gap-2 text-xs text-slate-500 dark:text-slate-400 mb-6 font-mono">
      <a href="/" class="hover:underline">Home</a>
      <span>/</span>
      <span class="text-slate-800 dark:text-slate-200">About & Methodology</span>
    </nav>

    <h1 class="font-serif font-extrabold text-3xl sm:text-4xl text-slate-900 dark:text-white mb-4">
      About & Methodology
    </h1>
    <p class="text-sm text-slate-500 dark:text-slate-400 mb-10 leading-relaxed">
      This page explains what this project is, where the data comes from, and — most importantly —
      exactly how the "constitutional sentiment" scoring works, so you can judge for yourself how
      much weight to put on it.
    </p>

    <section class="mb-10">
      <h2 class="font-serif font-bold text-xl text-slate-900 dark:text-white mb-3">What this is</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">
        The Executive Orders Archive is an independent, open-source research portal indexing every
        available U.S. Presidential Executive Order, from George Washington to the present day. It is
        <strong>not affiliated with the United States government</strong>, the Federal Register, or any
        political party. Nothing on this site is legal advice.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
        Every metric you see — reading time, readability grade, sentiment score, plain-English
        summary — is generated automatically by software, not reviewed by a human editor or a
        constitutional scholar. Treat it as a starting point for research, not a final verdict.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-10">
      <h2 class="font-serif font-bold text-xl text-slate-900 dark:text-white mb-3">Data sources</h2>
      <ul class="space-y-2 text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 list-disc pl-5">
        <li><a href="https://www.federalregister.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400 hover:underline">Federal Register API</a> — official presidential documents from 1994 to present.</li>
        <li><a href="https://www.presidency.ucsb.edu" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400 hover:underline">The American Presidency Project (UCSB)</a> — historical executive orders from 1789–1993.</li>
        <li><a href="https://www.archives.gov" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400 hover:underline">National Archives (NARA)</a> — supplementary historical records.</li>
        <li><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org" target="_blank" rel="noopener" class="text-blue-600 dark:text-blue-400 hover:underline">Wikimedia Commons</a> — public-domain presidential portraits.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-10">
      <h2 class="font-serif font-bold text-xl text-slate-900 dark:text-white mb-3">Reading time & readability grade</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">
        <strong>Reading time</strong> is a simple word-count estimate at 200 words per minute — the
        same rough heuristic used by most blogging platforms.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
        <strong>Flesch-Kincaid grade level</strong> is computed with the open-source
        <code class="text-xs bg-slate-100 dark:bg-slate-800 px-1.5 py-0.5 rounded font-mono">textstat</code>
        library over the first 10,000 characters of each order's text. It's a sentence-length and
        syllable-count formula, not a legal complexity assessment — dense legal text scores "hard to
        read" even when it's actually boilerplate.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-10">
      <h2 class="font-serif font-bold text-xl text-slate-900 dark:text-white mb-3">
        The "constitutional sentiment" score
      </h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">
        This is the site's most opinionated feature, so it deserves the most explanation. Each order
        is scanned for two sets of phrases:
      </p>
      <div class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-4 mb-4">
        <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-emerald-50 dark:bg-emerald-950/30 border border-emerald-200 dark:border-emerald-900">
          <div class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-emerald-700 dark:text-emerald-400 mb-2">
            Statutory Fidelity signals
          </div>
          <p class="text-xs text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">
            Phrases like "faithfully execute," "pursuant to statute," "authorized by Congress," or
            "protecting civil liberties" — language that ties the order to existing law.
          </p>
        </div>
        <div class="p-4 rounded-xl bg-rose-50 dark:bg-rose-950/30 border border-rose-200 dark:border-rose-900">
          <div class="text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-wider text-rose-700 dark:text-rose-400 mb-2">
            Executive Overreach signals
          </div>
          <p class="text-xs text-slate-600 dark:text-slate-400 leading-relaxed">
            Phrases like "notwithstanding any other provision of law," "without a warrant,"
            "commandeering state officials," or "suspend enforcement of" — language associated with
            bypassing Congress, the courts, or the states.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">
        Each matched phrase carries a hand-assigned weight. The weighted totals are combined into a
        single <strong>compound score from -1.0 to +1.0</strong>, which is then bucketed into
        <em>Constitutional Fidelity</em> (≥ 0.08), <em>Constitutional Friction / Overreach</em>
        (≤ -0.08), or <em>Constitutional Neutral</em> (in between — the vast majority of routine
        administrative orders land here).
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-3">
        This is a <strong>keyword and pattern-matching heuristic</strong>, not a legal analysis, an AI
        model's judgment, or a claim about any president's motives. It cannot understand context,
        sarcasm, or an order that recites "protects civil liberties" while doing the opposite. Two
        orders with near-identical substance can score differently if one happens to use more of the
        matched phrases. Please read the actual order text — linked and quoted in full on every order
        page — rather than relying on the label alone.
      </p>
      <p class="text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
        The "Framers' Constitutional Analysis" callouts and plain-English summaries on each order page
        are generated the same way: template-based text assembled from the same pattern matches, not
        original scholarly commentary.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="mb-10">
      <h2 class="font-serif font-bold text-xl text-slate-900 dark:text-white mb-3">Corrections</h2>
      <p class="text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed">
        This is an automated pipeline running against large public datasets, so errors — wrong dates,
        misattributed presidents, garbled OCR text from older documents — are expected. If you spot
        one, the underlying source is always linked from the "Original Source" button on each order
        page.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Support and Donation Section -->
    <section class="mb-10 p-6 rounded-2xl bg-amber-50/70 dark:bg-amber-950/20 border border-amber-200 dark:border-amber-900/60">
      <div class="flex items-start gap-3.5">
        <span class="text-amber-500 font-serif font-bold text-xl leading-none mt-0.5">★</span>
        <div>
          <h2 class="font-serif font-bold text-lg text-slate-900 dark:text-white mb-2">Supporting this project</h2>
          <p class="text-sm text-slate-700 dark:text-slate-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The Executive Orders Archive is maintained as an open-access public resource with no advertisements, paid tracking, or paywalls.
            If you find this research archive valuable for historical, legal, or civic research, you can support our ongoing server hosting and data processing costs via Buy Me a Coffee, Ko-fi, or GitHub Sponsors.
          </p>
          <a
            href="/donate"
            class="inline-flex items-center gap-2 px-4 py-2 rounded-lg text-xs font-semibold bg-amber-500 hover:bg-amber-400 text-slate-950 transition-colors shadow-sm"
          >
            <span>Support & Donation Options &rarr;</span>
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

  </div>
</Layout>
